// Copy to the repo root as `.oxlintrc.json`. oxlint parses it as JSONC, so the
// config carries its own rationale and these comments can stay.
//
// In a monorepo this file belongs at the ROOT, with oxlint as a root dependency.
// Without both there is no single oxlint to run from the root and nothing telling
// it where the plugin module is, so `bunx oxlint` pulls a stray version and lints
// with none of the architecture rules loaded — a green run that checked nothing.
// Nested `.oxlintrc.json` files load automatically for workspaces that need their
// own rule set (`--disable-nested-config` turns that off).
//
// Suppressing one line: `// oxlint-disable-next-line arch/db-isolation`. Position
// matters for some built-ins — `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` wants the comment
// above the dependency array, not above the hook call. Probe rather than assume.
{
  // One module, not one path per rule. It calls `definePlugin({ meta: { name },
  // rules })`; `meta.name` is the prefix every key below inherits.
  //
  // A bare package name resolves out of node_modules, so an ESLint plugin joins
  // the same array rather than needing its own mechanism — `eslint-plugin-sonarjs`
  // registers under the `sonarjs/` prefix. `jsPlugins` is also an `overrides` key;
  // reach for that only to scope a plugin to a subtree.
  "jsPlugins": ["./lint/oxlint/plugin.ts", "eslint-plugin-sonarjs"],

  // One `<root>/<dir>/**` entry per declared tree, naming its `generatedDir`, and
  // `harness/run-rule-fixtures.ts` fails the build when this list is not exactly
  // that. The names live in each tree's vocabulary; this file spells the globs
  // the names imply, the same way the `overrides` block below spells the tree
  // roots.
  //
  // This is here for the BUILT-IN rules. Every `arch/` rule already skips these
  // directories — `isArchitectureExemptSourcePath` reads the same `generatedDir`
  // list, in both tiers — but `typescript/` and `sonarjs/` know nothing about
  // that predicate, so without this an adopting project lints its own code
  // generator's output on day one. The two must agree: while only this pattern
  // read the list, `src/gen/client.ts` was a file oxlint never opened and a
  // blocking structural finding at the same time.
  //
  // Adding a hand-written path here is the menu re-entering: the guard rejects
  // any entry no tree's vocabulary implies, so widening it means declaring the
  // directory as generated, which is a claim about the tree rather than a rule
  // switched off.
  "ignorePatterns": ["src/gen/**"],

  // `typeAware` needs the `oxlint-tsgolint` dev dependency. Without it oxlint
  // aborts — "Failed to find tsgolint executable", exit 1, nothing linted — so a
  // missing dependency is a loud failure, not a config that quietly does less.
  // (Checked against oxlint 1.77.0.)
  //
  // Type information reaches the built-in `typescript/*` rules and nothing else.
  // JS plugins get no type checker, so neither `arch/` nor `sonarjs/` becomes
  // type-aware: every rule in this catalog stays a syntactic question about one
  // file, and no amount of this flag changes that.
  //
  // Type-aware linting is slower, and this config pays it: one config, full
  // safety, nothing extra for CI to know about. If pre-commit ever outgrows the
  // cost, the escape hatch is a second `.oxlintrc.staged.json` that `extends`
  // this file and sets `typeAware: false`, wired to the staged-file hook alone —
  // a narrower profile of one rule list, never a second rule list.
  //
  // `reportUnusedDisableDirectives` is read from the ROOT config only; setting it
  // in a nested workspace config buys nothing.
  "options": { "typeAware": true, "reportUnusedDisableDirectives": "warn" },

  // ── the shipped rules this catalog does NOT enable ──────────────────────
  //
  // oxlint bundles 847 rules — 14 plugins plus the unprefixed eslint core set — at
  // no dependency cost. Each `arch/` rule below was run head-to-head against the
  // closest of them, on that rule's OWN spec fixtures, with a control proving the
  // candidate was loaded. This block is the result: each shipped rule that speaks to
  // a subject an `arch/` rule owns, and the line saying why it stays off. Absent a
  // line, an adopter re-runs the search — and a rule missing from a config is
  // indistinguishable from one nobody thought about.
  //
  // SCOPE, because the paragraph above is exactly the kind of claim this file asks
  // to be checked. The head-to-head covers every tag, `types/` included. The `types/`
  // half ran second and differently: those rules want type information, so their
  // candidates are the 59 rules tsgolint implements, and the probe had to enable
  // `typeAware` and write a real `tsconfig.json` beside every fixture — without
  // either, all 59 report zero and every verdict reads as "covers nothing".
  //
  // It ran against the whole `typescript/` set rather than a shortlist: all 109 keys
  // oxlint accepts under that plugin, on each rule's own fixtures. That is a
  // superset of the typed 59, and oxlint REFUSES to start on a rule name it does not
  // know, so a config that parses is a config where every named rule loaded.
  //
  // The structural tier's subjects are covered too, in their own section at the end.
  // Those checks have no oxlint spec fixtures for a candidate to run against, so
  // those lines are arguments from what each built-in reports on the shared fixture
  // tree, not fixture-for-fixture verdicts like the rest.
  //
  // Nothing here is a taste verdict. In every case the built-in either misses a
  // spelling ours reports, or reports something ours deliberately allows — usually
  // the fix our own message prescribes, which is an edit loop rather than a second
  // opinion.
  //
  // Two facts that shorten every search below. First, oxlint ships NO
  // `no-restricted-syntax` and NO `import/no-restricted-paths`, so the AST-selector
  // and the zone-glob escape hatches an adopter reaches for first do not exist here.
  // Second, no shipped rule FOLDS `..` in a specifier before judging it — which cuts
  // less far than it sounds, and the difference matters: a `**` glob still catches
  // `@/shared/../infrastructure/db`, because it matches the unfolded string
  // literally. It is the exact-specifier `paths` form and
  // `import/no-relative-parent-imports` that miss it.
  //
  // THE RESTRICTED-* FAMILY, which is the generic form of about fifteen rules here.
  // `no-restricted-imports`, `no-restricted-properties`, `no-restricted-globals` and
  // `no-restricted-exports` are deliberately absent, together, for one reason worth
  // stating once: they ban a NAME, and every rule in this catalog bans a name IN A
  // POSITION. `no-restricted-globals` cannot exempt the env module that validates
  // `process.env` — there is one rule id for the whole global set, so an override
  // for that file also unbans `fetch` there. `no-restricted-properties` reads a
  // member access, so it misses an object-literal KEY entirely (all 11 of
  // `arch/no-disable-validation`'s violations) and bans `Effect.void` everywhere
  // rather than in the catch-handler position. `no-restricted-imports` reads a
  // static string specifier only: it DOES see `import x = require("stripe")` and
  // `await import("stripe")` — more than its name suggests — but not a
  // `require("stripe")` call and not a backtick specifier. Its exact-specifier
  // `paths` form cannot name a directory, while its `patterns` form is
  // gitignore-style, so a bare `stripe` matches any path segment named stripe,
  // including the wrapper our own message tells the author to import.
  //
  // Several of them DO reach fixture parity — but only once `overrides.files` globs
  // restate `isArchitectureExemptSourcePath` per rule, which is the copy this file
  // forbids in the `overrides` block below. That is the whole trade: the fence
  // becomes a dozen globs an adopter widens, instead of one vocabulary table.
  //
  // ── health, naming, testing ──
  // `no-nested-ternary` (core) and `unicorn/no-nested-ternary`: the core rule bans
  //   depth 2, which `arch/no-nested-ternary`'s header names as the budget on
  //   purpose; the unicorn one bans depth 2 spelled WITHOUT parentheses and is
  //   silent on `p ? 1 : (q ? 2 : 3)`, so it is the looser of the two and misses
  //   cases the core rule catches. NEITHER takes an option to raise the depth —
  //   `{max}`, `{depth}` and a bare number are all rejected as invalid
  //   configuration. Both also report the chain extracted to its own binding, which
  //   is the refactor our message asks for. Run as a pair, seven of our nine legal
  //   fixtures draw a diagnostic from them.
  // `id-denylist`, `id-match`, `id-length`: `id-denylist` compares the WHOLE
  //   identifier, so it bans `data` and allows `InvoiceData` — it reports none of
  //   `arch/no-vacant-symbol-names`' eleven violations. `id-match` cannot express the
  //   question at all: oxlint compiles its pattern with the Rust regex crate, which
  //   refuses look-around, and "ban these words" is inherently a negation.
  //   `unicorn/prevent-abbreviations` and `camelcase` are not bundled at all.
  // `jest/no-restricted-jest-methods`: the near miss of the whole catalog. Given our
  //   three method names it matches `arch/no-module-mocking` on all 17 fixtures, with
  //   our message text verbatim, and it beats ours on the namespace and aliased
  //   spellings. It stays off because the swap moves the method list out of
  //   fixture-guarded source into config: today dropping `unstable_mockModule` fails
  //   a spec, afterwards it fails nothing. A name list is legal vocabulary when it
  //   names the PROJECT's; this one names the rule's subject.
  // The rest of the mocking catalogue — `vitest/no-restricted-vi-methods` (the same
  //   rule under a second name, already drifted: no `unstable_mockModule` entry),
  //   `jest/no-mocks-import` and `vitest/no-mocks-import` (only `__mocks__/`
  //   imports), `jest/prefer-spy-on` and `vitest/prefer-spy-on`, and
  //   `jest/no-untyped-mock-factory` (which has no vitest twin) — all rewrite or
  //   narrow the mock ours asks you to delete.
  //
  // ── react ──
  // `react/no-multi-comp`: finds the same second component and then reports the fix
  //   ours prescribes — a compound component namespaced with `Object.assign`. Its one
  //   option, `ignoreStateless`, silences every function component, violations included.
  // `react/only-export-components`: a fast-refresh question, not a findability one.
  // `react/react-compiler`: its `EffectDerivationsOfState` IS `arch/derived-state`'s
  //   claim, but the rule is indivisible — taking it also takes `Purity`, which
  //   reports a `setInterval` effect that derives nothing. It is also blind to a
  //   setter called through a helper declared inside the effect callback.
  // `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` and `rules-of-hooks`: enable them as React
  //   correctness rules if you want them; they replace nothing here. `exhaustive-deps`
  //   sees exactly one async spelling, `useEffect(async () => …)`, and `rules-of-hooks`
  //   sees none of `arch/no-async-effect`'s thirteen violations.
  // `react/no-did-mount-set-state`, `react/no-did-update-set-state`: class lifecycle.
  // `max-params`, `max-statements`, `max-lines-per-function`, `vue/max-props`: none
  //   reports the number `arch/hook-count` and `arch/prop-count` report. A component
  //   takes one parameter, so `max-params` never sees a props surface; the size rules
  //   fire on the six-hook legal at any threshold that catches the seven-hook
  //   violation; `vue/max-props` reads Vue SFCs only. `vue/max-props` is also the
  //   precedent for a threshold being legal vocabulary — `maxProps` is its ONLY
  //   option, a number and nothing else. The other three are not that precedent:
  //   each pairs its `max` with a behaviour switch (`ignoreTopLevelFunctions`,
  //   `countVoidThis`, `skipComments`), which is the shape the `max-lines` entry
  //   below is rejected for.
  //
  // ── style ──
  // `react/forbid-dom-props`, `react/forbid-component-props`: they ban a prop NAME;
  //   `arch/no-inline-style-prop` bans the object LITERAL, so `style={styles.row}` —
  //   the shape it asks for — passes ours and fails theirs, and the primitives-layer
  //   exemption the header calls mandatory is unreachable without a glob.
  // `react/style-prop-object`: asks the OPPOSITE question, and fired on none of that
  //   rule's ten violations.
  // `react/forbid-elements`: catches the raw tags, but has no notion of the primitives
  //   layer or the root route, so it reports the two files whose job is to write `<div>`.
  // `no-magic-numbers`: a name collision with `arch/no-raw-primitives`, not a candidate
  //   — zero fires on its 38 violations. Against `arch/no-inline-font-size` it bans a
  //   VALUE wherever it sits, so it reports the token source that has to write the
  //   scale and stays silent on `fontSize: "13px"` and on every JSX attribute.
  // `unicorn/import-style`, `nextjs/no-img-element`: import FORM, and one tag already
  //   in `arch/no-raw-primitives`' element list without its exemptions.
  //
  // ── effect ──
  // `unicorn/max-nested-calls`: a depth count over all calls, so at `max: 1` it fires
  //   on the flat `App.pipe(Layer.provide(A), Layer.provide(B))` that
  //   `arch/no-nested-layer-provide` exists to ask for.
  // `typescript/no-unnecessary-type-arguments`: asks whether a type argument equals
  //   its parameter's DEFAULT. A correct `sql<Invoice>` never does, so it is silent on
  //   every fixture `arch/no-sql-type-parameter` owns.
  // Effect's own tooling, READ FROM SOURCE rather than run — neither package is a
  //   dependency here, and the language service is a TypeScript plugin oxlint cannot
  //   host. `@effect/eslint-plugin` ships two rules (`dprint`,
  //   `no-import-from-barrel-package`), neither in range. `@effect/language-service`
  //   ships 77 diagnostics, and two are near neighbours worth knowing about rather
  //   than replacements: `multipleEffectProvide` rewrites
  //   `x.pipe(Effect.provide(A), Effect.provide(B))` into
  //   `Effect.provide(Layer.mergeAll(A, B))` — the sequential shape
  //   `arch/no-nested-layer-provide` asks FOR, though it matches `Effect.provide`
  //   where ours reads `Layer.provide`, so the two do not currently collide; and
  //   `effectSucceedWithVoid` normalises `Effect.succeed(undefined)` to
  //   `Effect.void`, inside `arch/no-silent-error-swallow`'s void set. A project
  //   running the language service should expect both, and neither removes a rule
  //   here.
  //
  // ── types ──
  // ONE FINDING RUNS THROUGH THIS WHOLE SECTION, so it is stated once. The typed
  // built-ins keep prescribing `unknown` as the fix, and `unknown` in a declaration
  // is what this tag bans. `no-explicit-any` says "Use `unknown` instead";
  // `explicit-module-boundary-types` says "prefer `unknown` and narrow before use";
  // `consistent-indexed-object-style` says "Use a record type such as
  // `Record<string, unknown>`". Each of those sentences is a violation of
  // `arch/no-broad-parameters`, `arch/no-unknown-returns` or
  // `arch/no-opaque-record` respectively, written out in the help text. They are not
  // weaker versions of these rules; they are the step before them, and following
  // them lands exactly on the code this tag reports. That is an edit loop, and it is
  // why the overlap is only ever on the `any` spelling.
  //
  // `typescript/no-unsafe-type-assertion`: the strongest candidate in the catalog and
  //   the only one that covers a `types/` subject outright. It reports all four
  //   canonical `arch/no-widen-then-assert` flows — through `unknown`, through `any`,
  //   through `object`, through an open `Record` — on code that typechecks. It stays
  //   off because it is a BAN on narrowing assertions with no hatch, and this catalog
  //   deliberately took justify-over-ban there. It fires on `parsed as User` after a
  //   `JSON.parse` — the parse boundary `arch/no-widen-then-assert`'s own legal
  //   fixture blesses and `arch/require-safety-comment` exists to permit WITH a
  //   stated invariant — and on `el as HTMLElement`. Adding the `// SAFETY:` comment
  //   that `arch/require-safety-comment`'s message asks for does not silence it, so
  //   the two are not jointly actionable: the only edit clearing both is deleting the
  //   assertion, which makes the catalog's one justify rule dead. Measured reach, on
  //   typechecking code: every narrowing assertion reports; widening, `as const` and
  //   a same-type assertion are silent.
  // `no-unsafe-return`, `no-unsafe-argument`, `no-unsafe-assignment`, `no-unsafe-call`,
  //   `no-unsafe-member-access` — ON in this config already, and NOT candidates. They
  //   judge a VALUE of type `any` flowing somewhere; the `types/` rules judge a
  //   DECLARATION that names a broad type. The two never meet: passing an `any` into
  //   `function handle(payload: unknown)` is safe, so `no-unsafe-argument` is silent
  //   on exactly the parameter `arch/no-broad-parameters` reports. Both were run over
  //   both rules' full fixture sets and scored 0 on every violation.
  // `no-explicit-any`, `explicit-module-boundary-types`, `explicit-function-return-type`:
  //   the `any` spelling only, on every rule in this tag — see the edit loop above.
  //   The last two also report `arch/no-unknown-returns`' legal fixture, the inferred
  //   return it leaves to TypeScript on purpose.
  // `consistent-indexed-object-style`: reports one of `arch/no-opaque-record`'s two
  //   spellings and prescribes the other, whichever way it is configured — at its
  //   default it rewrites `{ [k: string]: unknown }` into `Record<string, unknown>`,
  //   and with `"index-signature"` it rewrites it back. It has no opinion about the
  //   VALUE, which is half the question.
  // `no-redundant-type-constituents`: reports `type T = unknown | string` for
  //   `arch/no-unknown-type-aliases`, and the fix is to drop the other members,
  //   leaving `type T = unknown` — the alias that rule is named for. Silent on the
  //   bare alias and on an alias chain.
  // `consistent-type-definitions`: reports the named type `arch/no-opaque-record`'s
  //   own message asks the author to write, because it prefers `interface`.
  // `no-unnecessary-condition`: the near neighbour of `arch/no-runtime-typeof`, and
  //   the one rule here reachable in config but absent from `oxlint --print-config
  //   -D all`. It asks whether a condition is statically decided, so on a genuine
  //   `typeof input === "string"` over `string | number` it is correctly SILENT —
  //   which is the case ours exists to report. Control-verified loaded.
  // NO CANDIDATE, and this is the load-bearing negative result: on a file holding the
  //   canonical violations of `arch/no-runtime-typeof`, all 109 `typescript/` rules
  //   together report NOTHING. `arch/no-opaque-record`'s headline
  //   `Record<string, unknown>` also draws nothing from any of them, and neither does
  //   `arch/no-known-value-widening`'s `const handlers: Record<string, Handler> = {…}`.
  //
  // ── the structural tier's subjects ──
  // `max-lines`: one threshold at one severity, and `overrides` REPLACES the entry
  //   rather than layering a second — `health/file-size` ships two, so a file can be
  //   finished and split in the same commit. It also lints tests and `.d.ts`, which
  //   the shared exemption predicate skips, and `skipComments` is an off-switch for a
  //   check whose claim is that every line counts.
  // `import/no-cycle`: reports cycles between FILES, one finding per participating
  //   file. `graph/domain-cycles` reports one finding per component, at the domain
  //   directory — and a domain ring can close through two files that import nothing
  //   back, where no file cycle exists at all.
  // `import/max-dependencies`: counts a file's import statements; `graph/feature-deps`
  //   counts distinct FEATURES one feature reaches.
  // `oxc/no-barrel-file`: forbids barrels, which this catalog requires.
  //   `naming/barrel-discoverability` governs what a barrel may SAY.
  // `import/prefer-default-export`: argues the opposite of that check, which bans a
  //   default export from a barrel because it leaves no name to search for.
  //   `import/group-exports` and `import/exports-last` govern where export statements
  //   sit in a file, which nothing here has an opinion about.
  // `import/no-namespace`: bans `import * as ns`; the shape that check reports is
  //   `export * as ns from`, which it never visits.
  // `unicorn/filename-case`: judges CASING. `naming/test-file-mirror` asks whether a
  //   test names a module that exists — and following the built-in's advice renames
  //   the file to something that no longer reads as a test, so the finding disappears
  //   without the test gaining a sibling.
  // `import/no-relative-parent-imports`: bans every `../`, including the three
  //   `boundary/import-policy` fixtures that stay inside their unit, and prescribes a
  //   move the layer grammar forbids.
  // `unicorn/no-empty-file`, `import/no-unassigned-import`: neither reads a function
  //   body, which is `health/trampolines`' whole claim.
  //
  // NEGATIVE SPACE ON THE NEGATIVE SPACE: oxlint opens no CSS and no markdown —
  // `oxlint -D all` on either reports "No files found to lint". So `style/css-tokens`,
  // `style/shadow-source`, `style/token-equality` and `health/doc-budgets` have no
  // zero-cost candidate by construction, and what follows was WEIGHED, NOT RUN — the
  // packages are not dependencies here and installing them was out of scope.
  //
  // stylelint could plausibly own `style/css-tokens` alone. There is no built-in
  // "must be `var(--token)`" rule; the routes are
  // `declaration-property-value-allowed-list` or the third-party
  // `stylelint-declaration-strict-value`, and BOTH take regexes as configuration —
  // the predicate knob, handed to the adopter, plus two to four new packages. It
  // cannot own the other two at all: `style/shadow-source` and
  // `style/token-equality` report on `.tsx` style objects and JSX props, and every
  // maintained CSS-in-JS syntax parses tagged template literals only. The
  // object-literal stack that once did (`@stylelint/postcss-css-in-js`,
  // `postcss-jsx`) is deprecated, and stylelint removed `processors` in v15.
  //
  // markdownlint cannot own `health/doc-budgets`: its nearest rule, MD013, is a
  // per-LINE character limit, and there is no word-count rule and no ratchet — a
  // ceiling that may only move down — anywhere in its index. vale and textlint
  // govern prose style, not document size.

  // The built-in rules, on for every file in the repo. Every `arch/` key sits in
  // the tree-scoped `overrides` block at the bottom, with the rationale for the
  // whole plugin.
  "rules": {
    // NEGATIVE SPACE: `typescript/no-require-imports` is deliberately absent, and
    // was looked at rather than overlooked. It bans the CommonJS *form* —
    // `require(…)`, `require.resolve(…)`, `import x = require(…)` — everywhere,
    // which is the cheaper half of the same fence: `arch/import-policy` already
    // reads all three spellings and judges where each one LANDS. Turning the
    // built-in on would make those visitor arms dead code and trade a verdict
    // about architecture for a verdict about syntax, including on the files where
    // a `require` is correct (a config read at load time, a script). A rule
    // banning CJS outright was proposed and rejected on the same ground.
    //
    // A project that wants the form gone too should add it as a style decision
    // and say so — not as a substitute for the policy, which would then be
    // enforcing nothing about those specifiers.

    // ── testing: a test exercises the module it names ──
    //
    // The ONE `arch/` rule in this block, and it is here for a structural reason
    // rather than by preference: its subject is test files, which every other
    // rule in the catalog treats as no subject at all. It reads no declared
    // tree, so a tree glob would not narrow WHICH files it judges — it would
    // just stop it seeing the suites that live outside one, the repo-root
    // `test/` directory included. `harness/run-rule-fixtures.ts` derives this
    // split from the rule sources and fails the build if a rule is in the wrong
    // block, in either direction.
    "arch/no-module-mocking": "error",

    // The type-aware built-ins that earn the tsgolint dependency, and only those.
    // Naming them is not decoration: `typeAware` on its own runs some at warning
    // severity — a green exit 0 with the violation printed — and leaves the
    // no-unsafe-* family off entirely.
    //
    // A promise nobody awaited is the bug agent-written async code produces most,
    // and it surfaces as an unhandled rejection nowhere near the line that made
    // it. The no-unsafe-* family stops a single `any` at an untyped boundary from
    // laundering itself through the call graph as a real type.
    "typescript/await-thenable": "error",
    "typescript/no-floating-promises": "error",
    "typescript/no-misused-promises": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-argument": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-assignment": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-call": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-member-access": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-return": "error",

    // Structural duplication and identity slips: both branches of an if/else
    // identical, a condition repeated in a chain, two functions with the same
    // body. This is the shape an agent leaves behind when it did not find the
    // code path that already existed and wrote a second one beside it.
    //
    // The duplication/identity subset only. sonarjs also ships complexity and
    // style opinions; those are taste, and taste does not belong in a gate.
    // Duplication too large for one rule's window is jscpd's job — setup/jscpd.json.
    "sonarjs/duplicates-in-character-class": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-all-duplicated-branches": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-duplicate-in-composite": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-duplicate-test-title": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-duplicated-branches": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-identical-conditions": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-identical-expressions": "error",
    "sonarjs/no-identical-functions": "error"
  },

  // ── the declared trees ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // Every `arch/` rule is scoped HERE, to the source roots this project declared
  // in `lint/policy/declared-trees.ts`, and the two lists have to agree: one glob
  // per declared root, spelled `<root>/**`. `harness/run-rule-fixtures.ts` fails
  // the build when they diverge, because a root declared in one and missing from
  // the other is a tree that reads as governed and is not.
  //
  // A monorepo lists one glob per governed root:
  //
  //   "files": ["apps/web/src/**", "packages/core/src/**"]
  //
  // and the alternative is a nested `.oxlintrc.json` inside each workspace that
  // `extends` this file — same scoping, expressed as file placement instead of a
  // glob. Nested configs load automatically; `--disable-nested-config` turns that
  // off. Either way the declaration list stays the source of truth, and the guard
  // reads whichever this file spells.
  //
  // NEGATIVE SPACE, and setup docs say it too: a tree not named here is a tree
  // this project did not adopt for. The rules are silent there — no findings, no
  // "unclassified" diagnostic — and that silence is not coverage. Adding a
  // package without adding its root leaves it unpoliced, and nothing says so.
  //
  // The scoping is belt AND braces: every rule named in this block also resolves
  // the file it is handed against the declared trees and returns early outside
  // them, so a project that reaches oxlint some other way still gets the same
  // silence. The globs are what keep those rules from being loaded for files
  // they will never report on.
  //
  // NOT every `arch/` rule — `arch/no-module-mocking` reads no tree and is
  // enabled globally above for that reason. The guard derives the split from the
  // rule sources rather than from a list here, so neither block can drift.
  //
  // `typescript/` and `sonarjs/` stay GLOBAL above. They are questions about a
  // file — an unawaited promise, two identical branches — not about a position
  // in an architecture, and a config file or a script wants them just as much.
  //
  // A project turning one `arch/` rule off for a subtree of a declared tree is
  // the menu this catalog exists to refuse. Where an exception is an
  // architectural FACT — a generated file, a test — it belongs in
  // `isArchitectureExemptSourcePath`, which both tiers read.
  "overrides": [
    {
      "files": ["src/**"],

      // Registration and activation are separate, and only one of them fails loudly.
      // A rule the plugin exports but this block never names is loaded and never run:
      // no warning, no output, exit 0. A key naming a rule the plugin does NOT export
      // is fatal. The typo is caught; the omission is not — which is why the spec
      // harness fails any rule missing from the plugin module.
      //
      // Keep each key equal to its file name (`lint/oxlint/boundary/db-isolation.ts` ->
      // "arch/db-isolation") so the diagnostic id is also the path to the rule that
      // raised it: `error arch(db-isolation): …`
      //
      // Every `arch/` severity here is read off the rule's own header rather than
      // decided here. A header opens with one of two labels, and the label IS the
      // severity: `Makes sure:` claims a property the codebase can rely on, which
      // only holds if the build stops when it breaks — `error`. `Shows:` reports
      // something a reader learns, which a build has no business failing on —
      // `warn`. The four warnings here are the four `Shows:` rules in the catalog.
      // The `typescript/` and `sonarjs/` entries carry no such label and were picked
      // one at a time; their sections above say why.
      //
      // NOT ENFORCED, either direction. `harness/run-rule-fixtures.ts` fails a rule
      // whose header carries neither label, and stops there — nothing reads the
      // severity column, so a header relabelled from `Shows:` to `Makes sure:`
      // leaves the value here stale and `bun run check` green. Re-derive the column
      // by hand when a label moves, until ea-49 makes the harness read it. The
      // guard that DOES read this file reads only the `files` globs above.
      //
      // A project that wants a warning to block edits the severity here and leaves
      // the header alone: the label says what the rule BUYS, not what this project
      // decided to do about it.
      "rules": {
      // ── api: what a unit's surface is, and who may name which half of it ──
      "arch/barrel-direction": "error",
      "arch/server-import-context": "error",

      // ── boundary: what may cross into a tree, and what may not leave one ──
      "arch/ambient-globals": "error",
      "arch/client-server-infra": "error",
      "arch/db-isolation": "error",
      "arch/import-policy": "error",
      "arch/no-test-imports": "error",
      "arch/route-thinness": "error",
      "arch/sdk-containment": "error",
      "arch/server-no-upward": "error",

      // ── effect: failures stay typed, services stay in the requirements ──
      // Four of these are silent in a project that does not use Effect: they test
      // for the `Effect.` or `Layer.` namespace before matching. Two do not, and
      // reach further than the tag name suggests — `no-sql-type-parameter` matches
      // any tag named `sql`, which is postgres.js and Kysely as much as
      // `@effect/sql`, and `no-disable-validation` matches any `.make(props, opts)`
      // carrying a `disableValidation` property. Both are still the right verdict
      // on those call sites; they are just not dormant, so read them as rules this
      // project takes rather than as ones awaiting an Effect adoption.
      "arch/no-disable-validation": "error",
      "arch/no-effect-catchallcause": "error",
      "arch/no-nested-layer-provide": "error",
      "arch/no-service-option": "error",
      "arch/no-silent-error-swallow": "error",
      // Turning this one off does not fall back to `arch/no-type-argument-assertion`:
      // it is the only rule that holds the `sql` name, so off here is off everywhere.
      "arch/no-sql-type-parameter": "error",

      // ── health: a decision is readable ──
      "arch/no-nested-ternary": "error",

      // ── naming: an exported name is an address you can search for ──
      "arch/no-vacant-symbol-names": "error",

      // ── placement: the one directory each kind of declaration lives in ──
      "arch/deprecated-paths": "error",
      "arch/no-deprecated-input-validator": "error",
      "arch/no-plain-export-in-server-fn-module": "error",
      "arch/no-raw-result": "error",
      "arch/schema-placement": "error",
      "arch/server-fn-placement": "error",
      "arch/server-fn-validation": "error",

      // ── react: state is derived, effects clean up, data comes through the cache ──
      "arch/derived-state": "error",
      "arch/no-async-effect": "error",
      // Counts, not properties. Each reports a number a reader uses to decide what
      // to split next, and a component that collects many hooks or many props by
      // design is not a defect.
      "arch/hook-count": "warn",
      "arch/prop-count": "warn",
      "arch/single-component-export": "warn",

      // ── style: every visual value names a token, and every vendor
      //    component is reached through its wrapper ──
      "arch/no-arbitrary-class-values": "error",
      "arch/no-inline-color": "error",
      "arch/no-inline-font-size": "error",
      "arch/no-inline-style-prop": "error",
      "arch/no-raw-primitives": "error",
      "arch/vendor-component-containment": "error",


      // ── types: no value arrives somewhere claiming a type nothing checked ──
      "arch/no-broad-parameters": "error",
      "arch/no-chained-type-assertions": "error",
      "arch/no-known-value-widening": "error",
      "arch/no-opaque-record": "error",
      "arch/no-reflect-access": "error",
      "arch/no-runtime-typeof": "error",
      "arch/no-type-argument-assertion": "error",
      "arch/no-unknown-returns": "error",
      "arch/no-unknown-type-aliases": "error",
      "arch/no-widen-then-assert": "error",
      "arch/require-safety-comment": "error",
      // A report on a spread that could have been written plainly, not a property
      // anything depends on.
      "arch/no-conditional-empty-object-spread": "warn"
      }
    }
  ]
}
